// @check
// @error-at 17:5 an `if` body requires braces: `if cond { … }`
// @error-at 18:6 expected `in` in for-loop
// @error-at 20:3 postfix `++` target must be an identifier
// Recovery is the POINT: one unbraced body must not cost the rest of the file.
// The arm is exactly one statement — never a scan — so the cursor lands back on
// the statement boundary and every later, independent error still reports from
// its own position. Before the recovery the first `if` swallowed lines 14-16 as
// its body and the run ended on a phantom `expected `}` but found end of
// input`; the two real errors below it were never reached.
//
// Typecheck-tier errors are suppressed HERE, and no longer everywhere: the
// `for x 0 to 3` mis-parse is a LOSSY recovery, so this file falls on the
// bailing side of the stage-1 rule (see `parser/lossy-parse-suppresses-
// typecheck.vl`). With only the unbraced `if` it would be typechecked —
// `parser/recovered-body-still-typechecks.vl` is that file.
let c = true
if c print(1)
for x 0 to 3 { print(x) }
let o = { x: 1 }
o.x++
